# Copy to `.env.development.local` and adjust. Committed as documentation — never put secrets
# here.
#
# NOT `.env.local`. That name is loaded in every mode, and @opennextjs/cloudflare reads the env
# files itself to build the Worker's runtime snapshot — so a `.env.local` on the build machine
# put http://localhost:4003 into a production artefact even when the build command pinned the
# live URL. `.env.development.local` is loaded only when NODE_ENV=development, which is `next
# dev` and nothing else.
#
# You may not need this file at all: src/lib/api.ts already falls back to
# http://localhost:4003 when the variable is unset. Create it only to point dev somewhere
# else, such as a tunnel or a staging API.
#
# The aylayoo-service REST API. Inlined into the client bundle at build time, so it must be
# set when you build, and setting it only at runtime does nothing for browser code.
#
# Local development (the Worker's dev port):
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:4003
#
# Production: the deploy paths pin the live API unconditionally — see the `deploy` target in
# project.json and the `cf:deploy` script in package.json. They used to read
# ${NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL:-<live>}, and that shipped a broken bundle: both Nx and Bun load .env
# files into the environment before running the command, so the variable was already set and
# the `:-` default never fired. To build against a different API, change those lines or use the
# `preview` target, which keeps the overridable form on purpose.
#
# The API only answers browsers from an origin in its own allow-list
# (aylayoo-service/wrangler.jsonc → vars.ALLOWED_ORIGINS): https://aylayoo.com,
# https://www.aylayoo.com, https://aylayoo-web.sbolorsaikhan4.workers.dev,
# https://ayalyoo-web.vercel.app, http://localhost:3000, http://localhost:5173 and
# http://localhost:8787. That is why `nx dev` pins port 3000 — on any other port the browser
# blocks every request as a CORS failure. Adding a front-end domain means editing that file
# and redeploying the *service*, not this app.

# No analytics variable here on purpose. Cloudflare Web Analytics is enabled for aylayoo.com
# through the dashboard's automatic setup, which injects the beacon at the edge after the Worker
# responds. Adding a second beacon in the app would double-count every page view, so there is
# deliberately no NEXT_PUBLIC_CF_BEACON_TOKEN and no <Analytics /> component. See the README.
